 ],
 "literal": "Even the calf not drinking, and not being poured into a vessel, / the sweet milk of a good cow spilling on the ground, / of no use to me, and of no help to my lord, / pallor wants to consume / the dark beauty of my waist adorned with beauty spots.",
 "translation": "Like the sweet milk of a good cow, which is neither drunk by its calf nor collected in a vessel but is spilled wasted on the ground, the dark beauty of my waist adorned with beauty spots is of no use to me, nor does it help my lord; instead, pallor desires to consume it.",
 "modern": "கன்றும் குடிக்காமல், பாத்திரத்திலும் சேகரிக்கப்படாமல், நல்ல பசுவின் இனிமையான பால் தரையில் கொட்டி வீணாவது போல, அழகுத் தேமல்கள் கொண்ட என் அல்குலின் மாமை நிற அழகு எனக்கும் பயன்படாமல், என் தலைவனுக்கும் உதவாமல், பசலை நோய் தின்று தீர்க்க விரும்புகிறது.",
 "explanation": "This poem is set in the pālai (wasteland/separation) landscape, spoken by the heroine to herself or her confidante in deep grief. She laments her husband's absence, which has caused her physical beauty to fade as she is consumed by pacalai (the sallow pallor of separation). She compares her fading beauty to the sweet milk of a good cow that is wasted—neither nourishing the calf nor collected in a vessel for others, but simply spilled on the ground. Similarly, her youthful, dark beauty (māmai kaviṉ) is of no use to herself in her loneliness, nor does it bring joy to her husband (eṉṉai) who is far away. The poignant image of wasted milk perfectly captures the tragic waste of her youth and beauty during their separation.",
